# Alembic migrations for QuantBlade

Single-database migration setup for the backend (SQLite via aiosqlite in the
app, plain `sqlite://` driver for migrations).

## Commands (run from `backend/`)

```bash
# Apply all migrations to the configured database
.venv/bin/python -m alembic upgrade head

# Roll everything back
.venv/bin/python -m alembic downgrade base

# Create a new revision from model changes
.venv/bin/python -m alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe change"

# Show current revision
.venv/bin/python -m alembic current
```

> **Always use `python -m alembic`, not `.venv/bin/alembic`.**
> This virtualenv was created at an older path
> (`/Users/lxxxx/Desktop/作业/智能选股/backend/.venv`), so every console script
> in `.venv/bin/` has a stale shebang and fails with
> `bad interpreter: No such file or directory`. The module form always works.
> The same applies to `.venv/bin/pytest` — use `.venv/bin/python -m pytest`.

## Existing databases (important)

The baseline targets **fresh** databases. A database that was already created by
`Base.metadata.create_all` (e.g. the in-use `stock_picker.db`) must be **stamped**
rather than upgraded, so Alembic records the baseline as already applied:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m alembic stamp head
```

`upgrade()` is idempotent — it skips tables and indexes that already exist — so an
accidental `upgrade head` against a populated schema will not fail or lose data,
but stamping is still the correct operation.

## Database URL resolution

`env.py` resolves the URL in this order:

1. `-x db_url=...` command-line option
2. `DATABASE_URL` environment variable
3. `settings.database_url` from `app/core/config.py` (i.e. `backend/.env`)

The async driver prefix (`sqlite+aiosqlite://`) is transparently rewritten to
the synchronous `sqlite://` driver, because migrations run synchronously.

## Baseline

`versions/0001_baseline_initial_schema.py` is the baseline that creates every
table defined in `app/models/`. See the header of that file for the exact table
list.
